\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Wilson time projectors at a site plane}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Menotti and Pelissetto fix the four-dimensional Wilson
action with Wilson parameter #{1}. Their field reflection
is antilinear, exchanges the field types, inserts the time
Dirac element and reverses Grassmann order. The observable
is gauge invariant and supported at strictly positive time.
The hopping parameter is strictly below #{1/6}; the
comparison here uses its nonnegative regime
\citet{(2.1)-(2.6), p. 370}{menottipelissetto1987positivity}.}

\p{Their link-plane discussion permits a Wilson parameter
between #{0} and #{1}, including the centered endpoint.
The site-plane argument instead uses the Wilson time
projectors to separate the boundary fields, then integrates
them through the positive spatial boundary matrix. The
bound on that matrix is the reason for the hopping
restriction
\citet{p. 371; (2.22)-(2.25), pp. 372-373}{menottipelissetto1987positivity}.
The argument integrates gauge variables, names no gauge
group and gives no finite periodic temporal prescription.}

\p{The two fixed slices of the [torus site reflection](fdip-007M)
therefore need their own boundary proof. The
[cellular sign model](fdip-00XN) instead swaps vertices and
fixes unoriented edges, and its full cochain action has
no Wilson time projectors. Neither geometric resemblance
identifies those quadratic forms with the source's one.}
